    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "How Do Laffer Curves Differ Across Countries?",
      "publisher": "Elsevier BV",
      "author": "Trabandt, Mathias; Uhlig, Harald",
      "published_at": "2012",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.2005876",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-10-18T15:40:29.844Z",
      "sha256": "44b52004412a0ec8a2d9206d1f60c11a48cbffa591b09201c00ba2703e9721f8",
      "rendered": false,
      "scraped": false,
      "excerpts": [
        "We seek to understand how Laffer curves differ across countries in the US and the EU-14, thereby providing insights into fiscal limits for government spending and the service of sovereign debt.",
        "As an application, we analyze the consequences for the permanent sustainability of current debt levels, when interest rates are permanently increased e.g.",
        "We build on the analysis in Trabandt-Uhlig (2011) and extend it in several ways.",
        "To obtain a better fit to the data, we allow for monopolistic competition as well as partial taxation of pure profit income.",
        "We update the sample to 2010, thereby including recent increases in government spending and their fiscal consequences.",
        "We conduct an analysis for the pessimistic case that the recent fiscal shifts are permanent.",
        "We include a cross-country analysis on consumption taxes as well as a more detailed investigation of the inclusion of human capital considerations for labor taxation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "The rainbow across the boundaries : a study of Leslie Marmon Silko's Ceremony",
      "publisher": "Montana State University ScholarWorks (Montana State University)",
      "author": "Yasuko Iseri. Idei",
      "published_at": "2004-01-01",
      "url": "https://openalex.org/W10331102",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-10-18T15:40:30.461Z",
      "sha256": "c9b0d1e5f6c17bdd47111dbe57965a741b528c48671b736212ae8d007ad47243",
      "rendered": false,
      "scraped": false,
      "excerpts": [
        "In order to fully understand contemporary Native American literature like the works written by Leslie Marmon Silko, one must have a sufficient knowledge of the Native American worldviews expressed in their oral stories that have been handed down for unremembered generations.",
        "The study has to include what the oral tradition has meant to the indigenous people and their communities, how it has been kept and passed down, and what it can do to the tribal peoples for securing their identity and power to cope with contemporary issues.",
        "Indigenous people have different worldviews from other culture groups; theirs are different in the conception of time and space, the importance of land, of the spirit beings, and the relationships with all the beings in Nature and in the universe.",
        "This study examines how Silko weaves tradition of oral storytelling and worldviews in her writing to pass invaluable messages across the boundaries of culture.",
        "Silko has a skill and knowledge ingrained in her blood to write from her tradition, and her works are not only compatible with the worldviews of the Native Americans but also she ingeniously expresses her messages in her works, including Ceremony.",
        "This makes Ceremony one of the most significant novels written in the twentieth century."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "Japanese Tea Culture",
      "publisher": "Directory of Open access Books (OAPEN Foundation)",
      "author": "Isao Kumakura",
      "published_at": "2023-01-01",
      "url": "https://openalex.org/W7137697807",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-10-18T15:40:30.461Z",
      "sha256": "f2744be96c175d24a92320ba2a4d1b3e3483eb5cabfe51962e1953ceffcaf028",
      "rendered": false,
      "scraped": false,
      "excerpts": [
        "How did the practice of “passing the bowl,” or mawashinomi, come about?",
        "And what hidden meaning lies behind the ritual purification of hands and mouth, or chōzu?",
        "Chanoyu, the art of preparing tea, developed against a backdrop of social turmoil in late medieval Japan.",
        "Through the singular figure of Sen no Rikyū, it found expression as wabi-cha, or wabi tea, the foundation of Japanese tea culture today.",
        "Here, scholar and curator Kumakura Isao investigates the unique cultural value of tea.",
        "He examines its rituals and behaviors, elaborates its structure, spaces, and style, and delves into the history of everything from the tea whisk to the tea room itself.",
        "Drawing on folklore studies and performing-arts history, Kumakura develops a new perspective on Japan’s culture of tea."
      ]
    },
